From b6787cb6036a0dcc7373fa084f2156ae30ab48b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 12:53:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08093/11362] [outer-loop] Inert-engine rejection on the induce
 path, behind a default-OFF flag

The 2026-08-01 generation taxonomy found INERTNESS -- an engine that predicts no
action changes anything -- is the largest single failure class of the live
generator (26 of 172 gemma-4-31B candidates, 15.1%; more than every code-validity
class combined) and the only class the induce path took no action on, despite the
detector being shipped and already imported at that call site.

WHAT SHIPS:
  * `engine_inertness_defect()` -- an `engine_inert` EngineDefect, bounded by the
    same killable subprocess the dry run uses. The precondition was real:
    `engine_changes_anything` is UNBOUNDED, and on the ft09 candidate behind the
    2026-07-31 13-minute wedge it does not return at all while
    `validate_engine_code` returns in 30.07s. Wiring the unbounded function would
    have reintroduced that hang.
  * `_run_isolated_job()` -- the spawn path extracted from
    `_dry_run_defects_subprocess`, so the second executor of generated code gets
    the identical process boundary rather than a hand-copied approximation.
  * `CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_REJECT_INERT`, DEFAULT OFF, read in
    `LocalGGUFProposer._engine_defects`. With it unset the path is behaviourally
    identical to before, so the control arm of the A/B is the shipped path itself.

`validate_engine_code` IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED: it is the definition
usable-engine yield is measured with, and folding the treatment into the outcome
would make that measurement circular.

TWO FAIL-OPEN DIRECTIONS, both asserted:
  * UNDETERMINED is not inert (killed at the bound / child unavailable / does not
    import) -- and the cases that really are the engine's fault are already
    rejected upstream by `dry_run_defects`, which runs first.
  * NEVER-RAN is not inert. Found by a test, not by review:
    `engine_changes_anything` continues past a raise, a None return and a
    wrong-shaped return, so an all-raising engine returns False from it and the
    first version of this labelled it "inert". `_change_census_inprocess` now
    reports n_usable_predictions so the finding requires positive evidence the
    engine ran.

Mutation-tested: all five new checks fail the suite when deleted. Two did NOT on
the first pass -- the ordering guard and the transitions-present guard were each
double-covered by another rule -- so the tests were rewritten around an engine
that isolates each (`MOSTLY_IDENTITY_ONE_RAISE`) and around asserting the probe
is not CALLED rather than that the result is empty.

FRESHNESS: seven artifacts record this module as a dependency. All four that
carry a rebuild_command were REBUILT from the code now on disk into a scratch
path (no committed artifact was written) and deep-diffed: zero substantive values
moved in any of them -- only wall-clock, code_provenance metadata, and
hand-added rebuild_note_* keys a fresh run does not re-emit. Acknowledgements are
APPEND-ONLY; the first attempt filtered same-path entries and deleted 12-94 lines
of acknowledgement history per artifact, which `git diff --numstat` caught as
deletions on an evidence file before anything was committed.

No default flipped. No scored or online ARC game played.

